you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  the uk government will set in law the  world's most ambitious climate change  target we hear what it'll mean for you  as well as big business  the young people campaigning to save the  planet and expecting world leaders  to take notice and environmental  activist erin brockovich tells sky news  the world  isn't acting quickly enough on climate  change  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and we aim to take you to the  heart of the climate crisis explain the  data driving the changes that are  already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come now today the uk government's  announced what it's calling a world  leading target to cut carbon emissions  by  78 by 2035 from 1990 levels  it's one of the most ambitious  environmental targets set out by a  developed nation  and takes the uk more than three  quarters of the way to reaching net zero  emissions  by 2050. so here's what it could mean  for you  everyone will need to eat less meat and  dairy whether that's cutting down the  amount that you have or committing fully  to a plant-based diet homes will need to  be much better insulated and people will  be encouraged to switch from gas  to low carbon heating the government's  banning the sale of new petrol and  diesel cars so there'll be more electric  cars on the road  and we'll all need to change the way we  travel by driving less and walking and  cycling  more air travel is expected to become  more expensive for frequent  flyers and short haul flights could be  banned where train or bus alternatives  exist  and couples may be encouraged to have  fewer children as a way of reducing  their carbon footprint one of the key  things the government will need to do  is match policies and practice with the  rhetoric of cutting emissions but  an initiative which was designed to help  people with things like insulation the  green homes grant was scrapped just six  months  after it was launched well our climate  correspondent lisa holland is a  at a company in birmingham which was  part of that scheme  so lisa how hard is it going to be for  the government to reach its target by  2035  it's a really ambitious target and it  should be good  theoretically for businesses like this  in birmingham which installs  insulation we know that about 20 percent  of our emissions in the uk  come from our buildings so we need to  better insulate them and stop  energy from escaping there was this  scheme the green homes grant  which was launched by the government  this business here said that it was  fairly chaotic  scheme and that's the knob of the  problem the policies the foundation  for getting to net zero uh has to match  that ambition  and the jury frankly is still out about  whether that will happen  lisa thank you well one of the key  things announced today is that for the  first time the uk's carbon budget will  incorporate the country's share  of international aviation and shipping  emissions well with me is our business  correspondent helen ann smith so hello  anne why is that important  well it matters massively because  aviation alone accounts for a huge  proportion of our  greenhouse gas emissions but big  business will be seeing this i think  today is a bit of a  challenge and an opportunity an  opportunity because they get to  potentially access new markets develop  new technology but a huge challenge as  well because particularly for those  bigger emitters some of them will need  to  radically pivot their businesses and  pivot them very fast and all businesses  will need to make  cuts back in 2018 for example businesses  alone  accounted for about 18 of our greenhouse  gas emissions so a big amount  and look while there are lots and lots  of strategies and blueprints and plans  on the table for how different sectors  are going to  cut their emissions what a lot of people  are saying is that when it comes to  business there needs to be a stick as  well as a carrot  the best example of this is probably the  automotive industry that new law that  we're now aware of  whereby you can't sell petrol diesel  cars from 2030. it was only once that  legislative thing came in that we saw a  huge swathe of  car companies moving to electrifly their  fleet so that really matters  hello nan thank you now those  commitments from the uk government come  as a new report from the international  energy agency has found  global carbon dioxide emissions from  energy production  are on course to surge by one and a half  billion tons  this year now that would be the second  largest increase in history  and here on our data dashboard you can  see how total co2 emissions  are ticking up all the time and they of  course have a big influence  on this number here human-induced global  warming  so here's those iea findings in a little  bit more detail  and you can see the change in co2  emissions  by fuel from 1990 up to  this year now the light blue color is  coal then  oil is in dark blue and finally gas is  in yellow and the drop that you can see  here is due to global lockdowns last  year because of the covert 19  pandemic but demand for all fossil fuels  is set to grow significantly in 2021  with global energy demand set to  increase by nearly  five percent this year however the iea  did find that renewables remain the  success story  of the covet 19 era and they're set to  provide more than half of the increase  in global electricity supply  in 2021 and on our data dashboard you  can see  the real-time power mix in great britain  now today  fossil fuels as you can see account for  54 of our energy  renewables 29 and nuclear  17 and according to the chief executive  of national grid  progress in renewable energy will be  crucial to helping the government meet  its net zero target  most people think only about 10 percent  by electricity comes from renewables  but actually in 2020 55  of all the electricity that we had in  the uk came from zero carbon generation  so if you compare that with just five  years ago for example so five years ago  about 25 percent of our electricity in  the uk came from coal  last year it was just 1.6 meanwhile the  national grid has developed a smart  low energy led light bulb that glows  green to signal when your electricity in  your home  is powered by clean or green energy  sources  it's part of a campaign to help people  make smarter decisions about their  energy consumption  they hope that people will choose to do  things like their laundry or  charging an electric car when they know  their energy is coming from renewable  sources  and you can see a more detailed  breakdown of the real-time energy mix on  our website  just head to skynews.com climate  in today's other climate news natural  habitats will play a crucial role in  getting the uk  to net zero emissions by 2050. that's  according to a report by  natural england which suggests certain  ecosystems can take huge amounts of  carbon dioxide  from the atmosphere researchers have  found a hectare of ancient woodland is  capable of storing the same amount of  co2  each year that would be emitted from 13  plane journeys  between london and rome  [Applause]  scientists are using neon coloured orbs  to investigate the  impact rockfalls have on the environment  test rocks the heaviest weighing 3200  kilograms  would drop down shroud bactobal mountain  in the east of switzerland  warmer temperatures thought permafloor  frost that's found on mountaintops  and it causes rocks that were previously  held in place by the ice  to loosen and fall downhill  and thailand is close to completing one  of the world's biggest  floating solar farms more than 144  000 solar panels have already been  installed on the surface  of the syrinthorn dam in one of the  country's northeastern provinces  authorities say it'll have the capacity  to generate 2725 megawatts of power  when it's complete  now on thursday president biden will  host the earth day summit  bringing together leaders from 40  countries around the world  and today a digital youth-led summit is  taking place to discuss  climate issues and form demands that  will be presented to the international  meeting  later this week ashna harinag has been  speaking to activists from australia  uganda and the u.s about their  experience of climate campaigning  [Applause]  a generation fighting for their futures  determined to not let their lives be  crippled by climate change  greta thundberg may be credited with a  school strike snowball effect but across  the world the climate voices  are getting louder and younger  sixteen-year-old anjali sharma  is taking the australian government to  court over a new coal mine  she says the burden of responsibility  weighs heavy on young shoulders  i think that while it is really  empowering you know like i  come home sometimes at the end of a long  day of um you know a school strike  meeting  or um you know the end of the press  conference and i feel really  accomplished but then it's also kind of  bittersweet because it's like  it sucks that i have to take my future  into my own hands from the front line of  the climate crisis  in uganda activist vanessa says many are  disconnected from a crisis  they can't see we are all  facing the same storm but we are  definitely  in different boats so meaning there are  communities that are  being affected right now the communities  whose bots are sinking faster or  burning faster but in the end of it all  if nothing is done about this then  everybody is going to catch fire  then every boat is going to drown  at 20 kevin is demanding cleaner air for  americans  he says his peers are vocal about  demanding change  because they care young people are  really making the connections between  women's rights  uh civil rights and environmentalism and  climate change  that's the reason why young people are  so heavily involved is because we want  to grow up in a world  where we don't have to be plagued by  these injustices these disparities  and inequalities as leaders of the  future their passion is driving the  climate movement  a generation of trailblazers determined  not to be drowned out  ashley harinad sky news  yesterday we told the story of what  residents call michigan's most toxic  postcode where locals have spent years  fighting the quality of the air that  they are breathing in  and you can see cordelia lynch's report  on the 48217 district on our youtube  channel or watch yesterday's episode on  catch up  now as part of that report cordelia  spoke to renowned environmental activist  erin brockovich whose successful lawsuit  against the pacific gas and electric  company  was turned into a hollywood hit starring  julia roberts  i know covet is our priority and right  behind it  should be the destruction  of this planet our water our air  and knowing that changes are coming and  we still won't get prepared  the conversation has to be had it has to  be  loud and it has to be all of us  how shocked and disappointed are you at  the kind of  status of america when it comes to  tackling climate change  on the global stage yeah i'm really  frustrated and you know i always  i really try to  listen understand respect  here both sides certainly the people  i've been doing this i started at 31  i'm 60. nothing's changed  do you think that america is confronting  environmental racism enough  no and i don't think we have been and  i've been out here for 20 years and  they're  at a disadvantage and they get taken  advantage of it's wrong at every ever  i'm see i'm not going to drop an f-bomb  and i almost did it's wrong  at every level and it sickens me  and i don't understand whoever those  powers to be why  it is you can't hear  the people who were down on the ground  drinking the water  eating the food living in these  conditions  frightened suffering and nothing  has gotten done we think what's  happening in mission may just be  happening in michigan  but it also happened in hinkley but i'm  here to tell you it's happening in maine  and it's happening in oklahoma  how comforting to you that america is  going to be able to catch up  quickly enough because it seems to be a  long way behind on this  for as daunting as everything sees i see  a watershed moment well that's  everything from us for today  on the daily climate show tomorrow how  planting seagrass off the coast of  plymouth  could help absorb millions of tons of  carbon and tackle climate change  that's at the same time here on sky news  see you then

